1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,954
Hello! Last time we learned the subway and buses.

2
00:00:02,954 --> 00:00:04,160
But once you arrive,

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,029
what if you can't find the way?

4
00:00:06,029 --> 00:00:08,200
Today we learn how to find your way,

5
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:10,370
here at Seongsan Ilchulbong in Jeju!

6
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,740
The first thought in an unfamiliar place: '여기가 어디예요'? (yeo-gi-ga eo-di-ye-yo = Where am I?).

7
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:19,889
(Where am I? ).

8
00:00:19,889 --> 00:00:23,490
The most basic phrase for asking where you are.

9
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:27,039
Even with a map, you're not sure.

10
00:00:27,039 --> 00:00:29,932
Then you say '길을 잃었어요' (gil-eul ilh-eoss-eo-yo = I'm lost).

11
00:00:29,932 --> 00:00:31,890
Don't worry — just ask!

12
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:39,353
'길' (gil = road or way) means a road or a way.

13
00:00:39,353 --> 00:00:43,888
'길을 잃다' (gil-eul ilh-da = get lost), '길을 묻다' (gil-eul mut-da = ask the way),

14
00:00:43,888 --> 00:00:49,020
'길을 건너다' (gil-eul geon-neo-da = cross the road). Today's core word.

15
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:51,346
To approach a stranger,

16
00:00:51,346 --> 00:00:53,875
start with '실례합니다' (sil-lye-ham-ni-da = Excuse me).

17
00:00:53,875 --> 00:00:57,220
This one word makes people happy to help.

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,588
'길 좀 물어봐도 돼요'? (gil jom mul-eo-bwa-do dwae-yo = May I ask for directions?).

19
00:01:03,588 --> 00:01:07,340
Ask politely and Koreans will gladly help you.

20
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:11,906
The magic phrase from last lesson returns: '어떻게 가요'? (eo-tteoh-ge ga-yo = How do I get there?).

21
00:01:11,906 --> 00:01:15,230
(How do I get there?). It works anywhere.

22
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,735
Add your destination: '성산일출봉 어떻게 가요'? (seong-san-il-chul-bong eo-tteoh-ge ga-yo = How do I get to Seongsan Ilchulbong?).

23
00:01:19,735 --> 00:01:23,820
Just swap the place name and it works anywhere.

24
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:29,497
Today's key word: '오른쪽' (o-reun-jjok = right) — right.

25
00:01:29,497 --> 00:01:31,743
This direction is '오른쪽' (o-reun-jjok = right).

26
00:01:31,743 --> 00:01:35,520
It comes up constantly in directions.

27
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:40,324
The opposite is '왼쪽' (wen-jjok = left) — left. '오른쪽' (o-reun-jjok = right) / '왼쪽' (wen-jjok = left).

28
00:01:40,324 --> 00:01:45,710
Learn them as a pair and you won't mix them up.

29
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:51,369
'-으로' (eu-ro = towards) marks direction. '오른쪽으로 가세요' (o-reun-jjok-eu-ro ga-se-yo = Go right).

30
00:01:51,369 --> 00:01:57,030
'왼쪽으로 가세요' (wen-jjok-eu-ro ga-se-yo = Go left). That's how you'll be guided.

31
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:58,288
But a problem!

32
00:01:58,288 --> 00:02:00,826
They spoke too fast and you didn't catch it.

33
00:02:00,826 --> 00:02:03,480
If you just nod and go, you'll get lost again.

34
00:02:06,070 --> 00:02:10,388
Don't be shy! '다시 말해 주세요' (da-si mal-hae ju-se-yo = Please say that again).

35
00:02:10,388 --> 00:02:13,410
This phrase is your lifeline today.

36
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:17,705
Even better: '천천히 말해 주세요' (cheon-cheon-hi mal-hae ju-se-yo = Please speak slowly).

37
00:02:17,705 --> 00:02:20,990
Then they'll speak nice and slow for you.

38
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,272
Key word! '똑바로' (ttok-ba-ro = straight) — straight.

39
00:02:25,272 --> 00:02:29,150
Keep going forward without turning.

40
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:31,810
'똑바로 가세요' (ttok-ba-ro ga-se-yo = Go straight).

41
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:34,053
'쭉 가세요' (jjuk ga-se-yo = Go straight) means the same.

42
00:02:34,053 --> 00:02:37,950
In real life you'll hear this one even more often.

43
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:42,023
Key word! '건너다' (geon-neo-da = to cross) — to cross.

44
00:02:42,023 --> 00:02:45,890
To go to the other side of a road or bridge.

45
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,551
'길을 건너세요' (gil-eul geon-neo-se-yo = Cross the road).

46
00:02:48,551 --> 00:02:51,989
Wait for the green light at the crosswalk,

47
00:02:51,989 --> 00:02:52,890
then cross.

48
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:56,783
'돌다' (dol-da = to turn) means to turn — to change direction,

49
00:02:56,783 --> 00:02:58,210
like at a corner.

50
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,618
'오른쪽으로 도세요' (o-reun-jjok-eu-ro do-se-yo = Turn right).

51
00:03:02,618 --> 00:03:07,760
Now you can combine direction words with verbs for full directions.

52
00:03:08,210 --> 00:03:11,941
'사거리' (sa-geo-ri = intersection) is an intersection where four roads meet.

53
00:03:11,941 --> 00:03:14,640
It's a key landmark in directions.

54
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:20,831
'신호등' (sin-ho-deung = traffic light) is another great landmark.

55
00:03:20,831 --> 00:03:25,120
'신호등에서 왼쪽으로 가세요' (sin-ho-deung-e-seo wen-jjok-eu-ro ga-se-yo = Turn left at the light) — Turn left at the light.

56
00:03:25,570 --> 00:03:32,241
People also use buildings: '편의점에서 오른쪽으로 가세요' (pyeon-ui-jeom-e-seo o-reun-jjok-eu-ro ga-se-yo = Turn right at the convenience store).

57
00:03:32,241 --> 00:03:34,090
You'll hear this a lot.

58
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:39,404
When you understand, say '알겠어요'! (al-gess-eo-yo = I got it!).

59
00:03:39,404 --> 00:03:43,780
And don't forget to say thank you — '감사합니다'! (gam-sa-ham-ni-da = Thank you!).

60
00:03:45,770 --> 00:03:49,200
You should also ask the distance: '얼마나 멀어요'? (eol-ma-na meol-eo-yo = How far is it?).

61
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:53,020
(How far is it?). Then you know if you can walk.

62
00:03:53,470 --> 00:04:00,507
The answer is one of two: '가까워요' (ga-kka-weo-yo = it's near) or '멀어요' (meol-eo-yo = it's far).

63
00:04:00,507 --> 00:04:02,780
Learn them as a pair.

64
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:08,595
They may answer with time: '걸어서 10분이에요' (geol-eo-seo sip-bun-i-e-yo = 10 minutes on foot).

65
00:04:08,595 --> 00:04:10,090
Oh, that's close!

66
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:17,650
'근처' (geun-cheo = nearby) means nearby. '이 근처에 있어요' (i geun-cheo-e iss-eo-yo = It's near here) — It's near here.

67
00:04:18,530 --> 00:04:23,672
For positions: '앞' (ap = front) is front, '뒤' (dwi = back) is back.

68
00:04:23,672 --> 00:04:29,190
'건물 앞에 있어요' (geon-mul ap-e iss-eo-yo = It's in front of the building) — It's in front of the building.

69
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:35,345
'옆' (yeop = beside) is beside, '사이' (sa-i = between) is between.

70
00:04:35,345 --> 00:04:41,580
'편의점 옆에 있어요' (pyeon-ui-jeom yeop-e iss-eo-yo = It's next to the convenience store) — It's next to the convenience store.

71
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:50,310
Use position words to make plans: '매표소 앞에서 만나요' (mae-pyo-so ap-e-seo man-na-yo = Let's meet in front of the ticket booth).

72
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:53,784
Finally! There it is — Seongsan Ilchulbong.

73
00:04:53,784 --> 00:04:54,980
We found the way!

74
00:04:57,790 --> 00:05:03,639
Now let's go up to the top. '올라가다' (ol-la-ga-da = to go up) means to go up.

75
00:05:03,639 --> 00:05:07,850
The opposite is '내려가다' (nae-ryeo-ga-da = to go down) (to go down).

76
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:11,424
Seongsan Ilchulbong has a LOT of stairs.

77
00:05:11,424 --> 00:05:13,310
Let's climb them one by one.

78
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:18,032
Honestly, it's tough!

79
00:05:18,032 --> 00:05:20,854
You can say '힘들어요' (him-deul-eo-yo = It's hard).

80
00:05:20,854 --> 00:05:23,130
Let's take a short break.

81
00:05:25,570 --> 00:05:27,942
'조금만 더'! (jo-geum-man deo = Just a little more!)

82
00:05:27,942 --> 00:05:31,297
) — what you say to encourage each other.

83
00:05:31,297 --> 00:05:32,360
Almost there!

84
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:37,440
We did it! '다 왔어요'! (da wass-eo-yo = we've arrived) means we've arrived.

85
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:42,335
Wow — the crater and the sea in one view!

86
00:05:42,335 --> 00:05:44,926
'경치가 좋아요' (gyeong-chi-ga joh-a-yo = The view is beautiful).

87
00:05:44,926 --> 00:05:47,060
It just comes out naturally.

88
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:49,615
But now it's reversed!

89
00:05:49,615 --> 00:05:52,094
A tourist is asking ME for directions.

90
00:05:52,094 --> 00:05:53,920
Time to use what we learned!

91
00:05:56,720 --> 00:06:03,009
Say it with confidence: '저기로 똑바로 가세요' (jeo-gi-ro ttok-ba-ro ga-se-yo = Go straight that way).

92
00:06:03,009 --> 00:06:05,380
Using today's word '똑바로' (ttok-ba-ro = straight)!

93
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:09,750
'그리고 왼쪽으로 도세요' (geu-ri-go wen-jjok-eu-ro do-se-yo = Then turn left).

94
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:13,320
Now you can use direction words freely!

95
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:17,048
'다리를 건너세요' (da-ri-reul geon-neo-se-yo = Cross the bridge). Perfect!

96
00:06:17,048 --> 00:06:20,830
Now you can both ask for AND give directions.

97
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:29,365
Let's review: '오른쪽' (o-reun-jjok = right), '왼쪽' (wen-jjok = left),

98
00:06:29,365 --> 00:06:36,668
'똑바로' (ttok-ba-ro = straight), '건너다' (geon-neo-da = cross). And '다시 말해 주세요' (da-si mal-hae ju-se-yo = Please say that again).

99
00:06:36,668 --> 00:06:41,050
You'll never be lost again!

100
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,126
Isn't the sunrise at Seongsan beautiful?

101
00:06:44,126 --> 00:06:46,490
Great work today. See you next time!
